::CW_U06_L02_Q01::Which author statement tone is most appropriate for a portfolio?{
~A. Defensive and dismissive.#Incorrect. A portfolio statement should not argue with readers.
=B. Reflective, specific, and confident.#Correct. This tone fits author reflection and publication context.
~C. Careless and vague.#Incorrect. The statement should be clear.
~D. Exaggerated and arrogant.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q02::Which sentence best revises "This story is about stuff that happened"?{
~A. Stuff happened and it was stuff.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
~B. I do not know what it is about.#Incorrect. This lacks purpose.
~C. Read it and guess everything.#Incorrect. This is dismissive.
=D. This story explores how a friendship changes when one character chooses honesty over comfort.#Correct. This states purpose clearly.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q03::Which author statement order is strongest?{
=A. Purpose, craft choices, revision or growth, reader effect.#Correct. This order organizes craft reasoning.
~B. Complaint, apology, random detail, file name.#Incorrect. This is not a useful author statement.
~C. Plot summary only.#Incorrect. Summary alone is insufficient.
~D. Font, date, file size, page count.#Incorrect. These do not explain craft.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q04::Which sentence sounds defensive?{
~A. I used repeated imagery to slow the reader down.#Incorrect. This is craft-focused.
~B. I revised the ending after feedback.#Incorrect. This is reflective.
=C. If readers do not understand it, that is their problem.#Correct. This rejects reader understanding instead of explaining craft.
~D. The final image is meant to feel unresolved.#Incorrect. This explains intent.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q05::Which sentence best communicates craft reasoning?{
=A. I used short lines so the final image would feel quiet and delayed.#Correct. This names a choice and effect.
~B. My poem is good.#Incorrect. This is vague praise.
~C. I wrote words on a page.#Incorrect. This is not craft reasoning.
~D. Nobody can question my choices.#Incorrect. This is defensive.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q06::A statement says, "I just wrote this and it came out pretty good." What revision is best?{
~A. I wrote this and it is perfect.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated.
~B. Nobody should read this.#Incorrect. This does not support publication.
~C. It exists.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D. I revised this piece to make the speaker's uncertainty clearer through repeated images.#Correct. This is specific and reflective.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q07::Which publication context requires a clear and respectful author tone?{
=A. A final portfolio or digital class publication.#Correct. These contexts require clarity and appropriateness.
~B. A private random note with no audience.#Incorrect. The lesson focuses on publication or portfolio contexts.
~C. A file name only.#Incorrect. A file name is not an author statement.
~D. An unrelated math worksheet.#Incorrect. This is outside the lesson context.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q08::Which statement preserves authentic voice while staying publication-ready?{
=A. I wanted the ending to feel quiet, so I let the last image do the work instead of explaining everything.#Correct. This sounds natural and craft-focused.
~B. This is deep and nobody gets it.#Incorrect. This sounds defensive.
~C. Whatever, it is done.#Incorrect. This sounds careless.
~D. I refuse to explain choices.#Incorrect. Author statements require explanation.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q09::What should be removed from an author statement?{
~A. Specific craft reasoning.#Incorrect. This should stay.
~B. Reader effect explanation.#Incorrect. This is important.
~C. Purpose statement.#Incorrect. This helps frame the piece.
=D. Empty or defensive language.#Correct. It weakens tone and clarity.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q10::Which sentence best explains revision or growth?{
=A. After feedback, I removed a sentence that explained the feeling too directly.#Correct. This names a purposeful revision.
~B. I did not change anything and will not explain why.#Incorrect. This does not show growth.
~C. The piece is perfect forever.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated.
~D. My draft has a file name.#Incorrect. This is not revision.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q11::An author statement is mostly plot summary. What should the writer add?{
~A. More plot summary only.#Incorrect. That repeats the problem.
=B. Purpose, craft choices, revision, and reader effect.#Correct. These elements create author reflection.
~C. A defensive comment.#Incorrect. Tone should stay reflective.
~D. Random punctuation.#Incorrect. This does not add craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q12::Which statement has the clearest reader effect?{
~A. Readers read things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The repeated porch light image slows the reader and emphasizes hesitation.#Correct. This explains the effect of a craft choice.
~C. The piece is there.#Incorrect. This is not reader effect.
~D. I like the ending.#Incorrect. This is preference.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q13::Which statement is too casual for a publication-ready author statement?{
=A. I threw this together and it turned out okay, I guess.#Correct. This sounds careless and uncertain.
~B. I revised the ending to create a quieter tone.#Incorrect. This is appropriate.
~C. I used first-person narration to keep readers close to the speaker.#Incorrect. This is craft-focused.
~D. I removed extra explanation after feedback.#Incorrect. This is reflective.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q14::Which answer best defines voice in an author statement?{
=A. The writer's personality or presence in the explanation.#Correct. Voice is how the writer comes through.
~B. Only spelling accuracy.#Incorrect. Spelling is convention, not voice.
~C. The number of pages.#Incorrect. Page count is not voice.
~D. Random formatting.#Incorrect. Voice is not random format.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q15::Which answer best defines tone?{
=A. The attitude the statement creates for the reader.#Correct. Tone is the statement's attitude.
~B. The file extension.#Incorrect. This is not tone.
~C. The number of paragraphs only.#Incorrect. Organization is different from tone.
~D. A private password.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q16::Which revision improves the tone of "My poem is amazing and nobody should question it"?{
~A. My poem is more amazing than everything.#Incorrect. This remains exaggerated.
=B. I used repeated imagery to create hesitation, and the final version asks readers to sit with uncertainty.#Correct. This shifts to reflective craft reasoning.
~C. I refuse comments.#Incorrect. This is defensive.
~D. The poem exists.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q17::Which statement is best organized?{
~A. I wrote it. It is good. The end.#Incorrect. This lacks organization and craft reasoning.
=B. Purpose: quiet uncertainty; Craft: short lines and repeated image; Growth: removed direct explanation; Effect: reader feels hesitation.#Correct. This follows the lesson order.
~C. Font, color, file, folder.#Incorrect. This does not explain creative work.
~D. A plot summary with no reflection.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q18::Why should an author statement avoid overexplaining every detail?{
=A. It should prepare readers without replacing the creative work.#Correct. The creative piece should remain central.
~B. Readers should be ignored.#Incorrect. The statement should help readers.
~C. Craft choices do not matter.#Incorrect. Craft choices matter.
~D. The statement should be confusing.#Incorrect. It should be clear.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q19::Which sentence best fits an author statement for a script?{
=A. I used stage directions sparingly so actors could show tension through movement.#Correct. This explains a format-specific craft choice.
~B. Scripts are words.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. I do not care who speaks.#Incorrect. Speaker clarity matters.
~D. Random spacing makes everything better.#Incorrect. Random formatting is weak.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q20::Which answer best revises vague praise? Vague praise: "My story is interesting."{
~A. My story is interesting because it is interesting.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~B. My story has paper.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
=C. The story creates tension by withholding the narrator's decision until the final paragraph.#Correct. This gives specific craft reasoning.
~D. Nobody should ask why.#Incorrect. This is defensive.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q21::Which checkpoint submission fits Lesson 2?{
=A. A four-sentence author statement with purpose, craft choices, revision or growth, and reader effect.#Correct. This matches the lesson requirements.
~B. A title only.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~C. A complaint about readers.#Incorrect. This is not reflective.
~D. A plot summary only.#Incorrect. Summary alone is insufficient.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q22::What should a writer do if the statement sounds fake or unlike the author?{
~A. Keep the unclear tone.#Incorrect. Revision is needed.
=B. Revise for authentic voice while keeping the tone reflective and specific.#Correct. The statement should sound like the writer and fit the context.
~C. Remove all craft reasoning.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required.
~D. Add defensive language.#Incorrect. This weakens tone.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q23::Which tone problem appears in "People probably will not get it anyway"?{
=A. It is dismissive toward readers.#Correct. The writer assumes readers will fail instead of explaining craft.
~B. It is too specific.#Incorrect. It is vague.
~C. It has too much evidence.#Incorrect. It has no evidence.
~D. It is overly organized.#Incorrect. It is not organized.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q24::Which sentence best connects publication format to author statement tone?{
=A. Because the statement will appear with final work, it should be clear, respectful, and specific.#Correct. Publication context affects tone.
~B. Publication means tone does not matter.#Incorrect. Tone matters in publication.
~C. Author statements should be random.#Incorrect. They should be purposeful.
~D. Format replaces meaning.#Incorrect. Format supports meaning.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q25::Which response best shows Lesson 2 mastery?{
=A. I can write an author statement that sounds authentic, uses a reflective tone, and explains craft choices with reader effect.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill.
~B. I can write only "it is good."#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. I can sound defensive and avoid evidence.#Incorrect. This weakens the statement.
~D. I can summarize the plot and skip craft reasoning.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required.
}
